{"name":"Amygdala Purple","hex":"#69045f","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"amygdala","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Each one of the two regions of the brain, located as a pair in the medial temporal lobe, believed to play a key role in processing emotions, such as fear and pleasure, in both animals and humans."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/amygdala"},{"term":"purple","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour between red and blue; violet, though often closer to magenta.","Any non-spectral colour on the line of purples on a colour chromaticity diagram or a colour wheel between violet and red."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a purple hue.","Not predominantly red or blue, but having a mixture of Democrat and Republican support."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/purple"}],"entries":[{"title":"Amygdala","blocks":[{"text":"The amygdala is a paired nuclear complex present in the cerebral hemispheres of vertebrates. It is considered part of the limbic system. In primates, it is located medially within the temporal lobes. It consists of many nuclei, each made up of further subnuclei. The subdivision most commonly made is into the basolateral, central, cortical, and medial nuclei together with the intercalated cell clusters. The amygdala has a primary role in the processing of memory, decision-making, and emotional responses. The amygdala was first identified and named by Karl Friedrich Burdach in 1822."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8b/Amyg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amygdala","wikidata":"Q338924"},{"title":"Purple","blocks":[{"text":"Purple is a color similar in appearance to violet light. In the RYB color model historically used in the arts, purple is a secondary color created by combining red and blue pigments. In the CMYK color model used in modern printing, purple is made by combining magenta pigment with cyan pigment, black pigment, or both. In the RGB color model used in computer and television screens, purple is created by mixing red and blue light in order to create colors that appear similar to violet light. According to color theory, purple is considered a cool color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purple","wikidata":"Q3257809"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/Amigdale1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/Amigdale1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmigdale1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Constudoverbrain.png/330px-Constudoverbrain.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Constudoverbrain.png/330px-Constudoverbrain.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Human brain in the coronal orientation. Amygdalae are shown in dark red.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AConstudoverbrain.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Mouse_Amygdala.pdf/page1-500px-Mouse_Amygdala.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Mouse_Amygdala.pdf/page1-500px-Mouse_Amygdala.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Subdivisions of the mouse amygdala","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMouse_Amygdala.pdf"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8a/Amygdala_coronal_sections.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8a/Amygdala_coronal_sections.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmygdala_coronal_sections.gif"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Amygdala_sagittal_sections.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Amygdala_sagittal_sections.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmygdala_sagittal_sections.gif"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/07/Amygdala_transversal_sections.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/07/Amygdala_transversal_sections.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmygdala_transversal_sections.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Gray_718-amygdala.png/500px-Gray_718-amygdala.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Gray_718-amygdala.png/500px-Gray_718-amygdala.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Coronal section of brain through intermediate mass of third ventricle. Amygdala is shown in purple.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray_718-amygdala.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8